{#
  BGP peering for EVPN - the session only.

  What it carries is the VTEP block: the node takes leaf1's route for
  {{ udn_bgp_vtep_prefix }}.0/24 (toReceive), which is how its VXLAN packets
  reach the other VTEPs - leaf1 holds a static /32 per node VTEP behind that
  aggregate. Without it the EVPN routes appear on both sides and no tunnel
  ever forms, because neither end can reach the other's tunnel endpoint. That
  looks exactly like a data-plane problem and is not one.

  It does NOT activate l2vpn evpn, and cannot: the FRRConfiguration neighbor
  has no address-family field (`oc explain
  frrconfiguration.spec.bgp.routers.neighbors` - dualStackAddressFamily is
  IPv4+IPv6 unicast). An earlier version of this file carried
  `addressFamilies: [unicast, evpn]` and `allowAsIn: origin`; the API server
  pruned both without a word, and they were only ever visible in the
  last-applied-configuration annotation. OVN-Kubernetes activates the EVPN
  family itself, in the ovnk-generated-* FRRConfigurations it writes once a
  RouteAdvertisements selects an EVPN network and names this configuration
  (by the label below): `neighbor <leaf1> activate`, `allowas-in origin` and
  `advertise-all-vni` under `address-family l2vpn evpn`, and `allowas-in
  origin` under ipv4 unicast too - which is what the pruned allowAsIn was for
  (every node of a cluster shares its ASN). Until then leaf1 shows this neighbor as NoNeg in
  `show bgp l2vpn evpn summary`, which is correct. See troubleshooting.md,
  "NoNeg after the EVPN peering".

  Those generated configurations also carry the VNIs, route distinguishers
  and route targets, from each CUDN's network.evpn block. Do not hand-write
  those here; two sources for the same VNI is how you get a tunnel that forms
  and carries nothing.
#}
apiVersion: frrk8s.metallb.io/v1beta1
kind: FRRConfiguration
metadata:
  name: fabric-peering-evpn
  namespace: {{ udn_bgp_frrk8s_namespace }}
  labels:
    {{ udn_bgp_frr_label_key }}: fabric-evpn
spec:
  bgp:
    routers:
      - asn: {{ udn_bgp_cluster_asn }}
        neighbors:
          - address: {{ clab_fabric_prefix }}.{{ clab_fabric_router_octet }}
            asn: {{ clab_leaf1_asn }}
{% if udn_bgp_password | default('') | length > 0 %}
            # TCP-MD5 on this session. Emitted only when udn_bgp_password is
            # set in vault.yaml; with it unset this block is absent entirely
            # and the manifest is byte-identical to an unauthenticated lab.
            #
            # passwordSecret, not password: the plain field would put the key
            # into this rendered file under udn-bgp/, which is on disk in the
            # clear. The CRD makes them mutually exclusive, so it is one or
            # the other and this is the one worth having.
            passwordSecret:
              name: {{ udn_bgp_password_secret }}
              namespace: {{ udn_bgp_frrk8s_namespace }}
{% endif %}
            holdTime: 9s
            keepaliveTime: 3s
            port: 179
            toReceive:
              allowed:
                mode: all
            toAdvertise:
              allowed:
                # The VTEP block is what the far end needs in order to build
                # a tunnel back to this node. It is advertised explicitly
                # rather than with mode 'all', which would also put
                # {{ lab_network_prefix }}.0/24 - the lab's management
                # network - into the fabric.
                prefixes:
                  - {{ udn_bgp_vtep_prefix }}.0/24
        prefixes:
          - {{ udn_bgp_vtep_prefix }}.0/24
  nodeSelector: {}
